{
 "name": "Alapin Sicilian (2.c3)",
 "eco": "B22",
 "side": "white",
 "summary": "1.e4 c5 2.c3. You prepare d4 so you can keep a pawn centre. Five shapes cover Black's replies: 2...d5, 2...Nf6, 2...e6, 2...d6, and 2...g6 (with 2...Nc6 and 2...e5).",
 "coach_note": "Your first Alapin game was a win: Jetblack007 vs leehooni (1753), Lichess rapid 10+5, Oct 22, 2026. Black locked the queenside with ...c4 and went ...Bf5 and ...Bd3. You kept the edge. Two lessons from that game are here: Nf4 and Nh5 to trade the f6 knight, and trade queens first when you are ahead.",
 "coach_link": {
  "label": "Open your first Alapin game (1-0) ↗",
  "href": "https://lichess.org/XtnIByaY"
 },
 "show_tactic_links": true,
 "scheme": [
  "2.c3 prepares d4, so you can take back on d4 with a pawn and keep a full centre.",
  "Play d4 on move three, unless Black has hit e4. Then play e5 against ...Nf6 and exd5 against ...d5.",
  "Develop: Nf3, a bishop, castle. Then choose between the isolated-pawn attack (Ne5, Bd3, Re1) and a space squeeze.",
  "When you are ahead, trade queens first (Mistake Deck card: 'Trade queens first when ahead')."
 ],
 "reading": [
  "Black's second move names the set-up. ...d5 and ...Nf6 hit e4 at once. ...e6 and ...d6 wait. ...g6 aims at d4.",
  "A black pawn on c4 (the ...c4 lock) means the fight moves to the kingside and the d3 and e4 squares.",
  "If the f6 knight is Black's only kingside defender, look at Nh5 or Bxf6 to remove it."
 ],
